  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (kidney/renal cancer, including the clinically dominant subtype renal cell carcinoma). Among them, PGS004908 has the strongest visible, endpoint-aligned evidence package: phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Kidney cancer”, covariates are standard risk-adjustment (age, sex, PCs, BMI, smoking, hypertension), and it reports the highest AUROC in the set (full_model_auc=0.74 with AUROC 0.74 [0.72–0.75]) alongside a strong per-SD effect size (OR=1.5 [1.43–1.58]) with very large European evaluation (n=324,805). Competing models either have lower reported AUROC (e.g., PGS000722 0.567; PGS004690 0.5644; PGS000605/606 0.517), lack comparable discrimination metrics (e.g., PGS000161, PGS003744, PGS004245), or have similar AUROC but less compelling metadata (PGS000076/PGS000787 report 0.722 but without clear PRS-only metrics and with covariate-heavy full-model framing). Main limitation: the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (no explicit PRS-only AUROC/incremental AUROC), so the PRS-specific contribution cannot be cleanly compared across candidates, lowering confidence from High."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 candidates are direct matches to kidney cancer (or near-synonymous renal cancer/renal carcinoma/renal cell carcinoma), so selection is based on endpoint fidelity plus the strongest mutually consistent performance/validation evidence available. PGS004908 reports kidney cancer with renal carcinoma EFO, uses genome-wide significant SNPs (107 variants) from a disease-focused kidney cancer GWAS (Nat Genet, multi-ancestry discovery) and has the strongest reported discrimination among the listed models (full-model AUROC 0.74 with CI 0.72–0.75) together with very large European evaluation (n=324,805) and relevant covariates (age, sex, PCs, BMI, smoking, hypertension). Competing models have either lower reported AUROC (e.g., PGS000722 AUROC 0.567; PGS004690 AUROC 0.5644; PGS000605/606 AUROC 0.517) or lack comparable AUC/R2 (e.g., PGS000161, PGS003744, PGS004245). Limitation: the reported AUROCs here are full-model metrics (not explicitly PGS-only), and covariate sets differ across studies; therefore the apparent AUROC advantage may partly reflect covariate contribution rather than PRS contribution, which prevents a high-confidence, high-quality designation."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct/near-direct matches to the target concept (nodular goiter), with two UKB portability-framework models labeled as nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three disease-focused benign nodular goiter models (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional candidates (PGS005273, PGS005274) are contrast models for thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less endpoint-faithful for generic nodular goiter risk.

PGS005262 is preferred because it is explicitly aligned to the target as a benign nodular goiter diagnosis (trait_reported/trait_efo and phenotyping_reported indicate benign nodular goiter) and comes from a disease-focused, multi-cohort thyroid disease study context with large validation support (n=94,651) and the best reported discrimination among the benign-goiter models (full_model AUROC=0.5933; effect size OR per SD ~1.41). 

However, confidence is not high because key PRS-comparable performance metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2, incremental metrics) are not provided and the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric with unknown covariates, limiting cross-model comparability; additionally, the nontoxic multinodular goiter portability models report only small partial correlations in EUR validation (0.024–0.0277), making the performance evidence across model families difficult to reconcile cleanly."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004944",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 5 candidates are direct matches to open-angle glaucoma via trait_efo and have phenotyping_reported consistent with primary open-angle glaucoma, but they differ in endpoint fidelity and interpretability of reported performance. PGS004944 is best supported overall because it has (i) direct phenotype alignment (primary open-angle glaucoma; explicitly noted as self-reported), (ii) the strongest visible validation support (n=407,667) with multi-ancestry evaluation listed, and (iii) supportive discrimination signals (reported AUROC 0.748 and OR 1.74). However, confidence is not high because the AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (covariates include age/age^2/sex/ancestry) rather than an explicitly PRS-only AUROC, and endpoint ascertainment is self-reported; additionally, PGS001797 reports a comparable full-model AUROC (0.749) plus a PRS-comparable R2 (0.0315), but comes from a global biobank framework context and has much smaller validation (n=7,128). These limitations keep the selection at DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL rather than DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005217","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct/near-direct matches to peripheral vascular disease (PVD), split between a broad PVD-ICD style endpoint (PGS001843, PGS002055: “Peripheral vascular disease, unspecified”) and peripheral artery disease (PAD) endpoints (PGS005158, PGS005217). For a generic PVD target, endpoint fidelity is imperfect across the set because PAD is a closely related but not identical label, and the two exact-label PVD scores come from a portability framework paper.

PGS005217 is preferred because it is disease-focused on incident PAD with large external validation (validation n=304,294), multi-ancestry evaluation listed, and reports a strong PRS effect size (HR per SD 1.66 [1.61–1.71]) with relatively clean covariates (age, sex, PCs). In contrast, PGS001843/PGS002055 are from the “Portability of 245 polygenic scores…” framework and only report weak PRS signal via partial correlation (partial-r 0.0151 and 0.0175, respectively) despite large UKB-based development; this package provides limited disease-specific support. PGS005158 is also PAD but uses only 19 genome-wide significant SNPs, has smaller validation (n=10,836), and includes heavy clinical/lifestyle covariates, making its reported HR less comparable as a PRS-quality signal.

Main limitation: no PRS-only AUROC/R2 is available for PGS005217 (only a full-model C-index is shown), and PAD is not an exact textual match to the broader PVD concept, so confidence is constrained and the selection remains sub-optimal given the endpoint/metric comparability gaps."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004908","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 8 candidates are direct matches conceptually (renal carcinoma / kidney cancer / renal cell carcinoma) with diagnosis-style endpoints (e.g., “Kidney cancer”, “Renal cancer”, often incident). Per the provided domain guidance, renal cell carcinoma can be treated as the dominant subtype of kidney cancer, so RCC-labeled models remain acceptable matches for a renal carcinoma target.

Among the set, PGS004908 has the strongest visible, directly comparable performance evidence in European evaluation (AUROC 0.74, CI 0.72–0.75; OR 1.5, CI 1.43–1.58) with a very large validation sample (n=324,805) and covariates that appear to be standard demographic/PC plus common risk factors (age, sex, PCs, BMI, smoking, hypertension). Other large-cohort candidates either report lower AUROC (e.g., PGS000722 ~0.567; PGS004690 ~0.5644) or report only full-model AUROC around 0.722 without PRS-only metrics (PGS000076/PGS000787), making cross-model PRS contribution comparability uncertain.

Main limitation: the best AUROC shown for PGS004908 is still a full-model metric (no explicit PRS-only AUROC/R2 is provided), so the degree to which covariates vs PRS drive discrimination cannot be isolated from the current metadata; thus confidence is Moderate rather than High."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000209",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to the target concept (trait_efo: thyroid carcinoma; trait_reported: thyroid cancer), but most report only full-model AUROC (often with substantial covariates) and few provide PRS-only metrics, limiting comparability. Among the disease-focused, thyroid-cancer-specific PNAS scores, PGS000208 shows the highest AUROC (0.751) but is reported for a model including family history (and other covariates), making the AUROC less interpretable as PRS performance; several other candidates are from broader framework/repository efforts (e.g., snpnet/PRS-CS-auto/PRSweb), which the provided domain guidance cautions against preferring when strong disease-focused alternatives exist. PGS000209 is selected because it remains within the same dedicated thyroid-cancer study family, uses a direct thyroid cancer endpoint, reports comparable discrimination (AUROC 0.694 with CI), and has the largest validation sample size among the PNAS set (n=408,479) with a cleaner covariate set (gender, birth year) than the family-history–augmented variant, improving comparability. Remaining limitation: performance evidence is still largely full-model AUROC rather than explicit PRS-only metrics, and multiple other direct-match candidates (including multi-ancestry 2026 preprint PRSCS models and other large-cohort evaluations) cannot be cleanly ranked against it from the visible metadata alone."
}
